format - make a new filesystem on a volume

  format hd0p1
  format -t fat16 usb0p1

Writes an empty FAT filesystem. All data on the volume is lost.

The width (fat12, fat16, fat32) is chosen from the volume's size unless -t
names one. A volume too small for the named width is refused.

Refused on a mounted volume. Asks for the volume name before writing.
`disk` lists volumes.
